# .gitignore for db2's git-init'd /srv/ (repo root is /srv/, not /srv/docker/
# -- other /srv siblings exist: cron/, docker-replicated/, glusterfs/).
# Host-specific -- see gitignore-db1 for db1's separate copy; do not merge
# the two, since a finding specific to one host (a stale duplicate directory,
# a data-path only that host has) can wrongly exclude something the other
# host actually needs tracked, or leave a no-op pattern for something that
# never existed there in the first place. `git init` runs from /srv/ itself,
# not /srv/docker/.
#
# ---- scope: only docker/ is tracked, everything else at /srv root is ignored ----
# cron/, docker-replicated/, glusterfs/ (and anything else that shows up at the
# /srv root later) are out of scope for this repo entirely -- not audited, not
# reviewed for secrets, just excluded outright. Revisit deliberately if this
# repo's scope is ever meant to grow beyond docker/.
/*
!/docker/
!/.gitignore
!/.githooks/

# ---- everything below only ever applies inside docker/, since that's the only
#      top-level entry not excluded above ----
#
# This file holds only host-wide GENERIC rules (secret file patterns, runtime
# data, stale directories, nested repos). It deliberately does NOT list any
# specific per-service file -- those live in a per-directory .gitignore
# instead (e.g. /srv/docker/gitlab-runner/.gitignore containing just
# "config.toml"), one level down, right next to the file it excludes. That's
# because db1 and db2 can have same-named service directories (watchtower,
# restic, patroni, gitlab-runner, ...) whose actual content differs -- a file
# secret-bearing on one host may already be clean on the other. A
# per-directory .gitignore is self-contained and host-specific by
# construction; a shared root-level list of specific paths is not, and
# copying one host's findings into the other's repo would wrongly exclude a
# file that host never needed excluded.
#
# sanitize_service_files.py --write generates each service's .gitignore for
# you automatically (only for services where it actually found something to
# redact) -- review its output and copy it to
# /srv/docker/<service>/.gitignore. This root file only needs manual edits
# for genuinely host-wide patterns or genuinely root-level (/docker/<file>)
# loose files.

# ---- secrets: never track ----
.env
.env.*
*.env
*.env_*
*.key
*.pem
*.pfx
*.p12
*.der
htpasswd
users.acl
ldap.toml

# ---- known secret-bearing loose file directly at /srv/docker root (not inside
#      any service directory, so a per-directory .gitignore doesn't apply).
#      Anchored to that exact path -- NOT a bare "config.toml" pattern, which
#      would also wrongly exclude an unrelated, harmless config.toml that some
#      other service might legitimately have.
#      NOTE: .gitignore does NOT support trailing inline comments -- a "#"
#      after a pattern on the same line is parsed as part of the pattern
#      itself (making it match nothing), not stripped as a comment. Comments
#      must be on their own line, before the pattern. (A real file here, with
#      a runner-registration token in it -- not the same as db1's config.toml,
#      which is an empty directory.) ----
/docker/config.toml

# ---- runtime data / logs / backups: not config, don't track ----
*.log
*.rdb
data/
restored-data/
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tar.bz2

# ---- nested repos: managed by their own .git, not this one ----
# dockprom/ and zulip/ already have their own .git (customized clones of
# upstream projects) — excluded here so the top-level repo doesn't treat them
# as embedded repos/gitlinks. Commit their local changes
# (docker-compose.override.yaml, prometheus-customized.yaml,
# compose.override.yaml, etc.) inside each directory itself:
# `cd docker/dockprom && git add/commit` (same for zulip/).
dockprom/
zulip/

# ---- live production Postgres HA cluster: root-only .env by design, handled
#      by hand, as root, not through the bulk/scripted pass. ----
pg-cluster-instance-2/

# ---- stale/duplicate/abandoned directories, ignored here as a fallback ----
# (clickhouse-external-old/, gitlab-ee-failed/, gittlab-runner/, *.back.yaml,
#  *.bak, *.new.yaml -- better to `rm` these than carry them forever, but not
#  this tool's call to make)
# NOTE: gitignore patterns are case-sensitive on Linux -- "*.bak" alone missed
# a real "CA.pem.BAK" file during review. Bracket expressions below match either case.
*.[bB][aA][kK]
*-old/
*_old/
*test/
*failed/
gittlab-runner/
*.back.yaml
*.new.yaml

# ---- cert/key material directories: never track ----
# e.g. pg-cluster-instance-2/certs/, certs_old/, pki/, redis-staging/tmp-conf/
certs/
certs_old/
pki/
tmp-conf/

# ---- known junk/mistake files, left in place on disk, just not tracked ----
# Literally named ".crt"/".csr" (empty basename) -- almost certainly leftover
# from a botched command. Not deleted (that's a deliberate choice, not this
# tool's call to make); anchored exact-path exclusion, NOT a blanket *.crt/*.csr
# pattern, since real certs elsewhere in proxy/ are meant to stay tracked.
/docker/proxy/.crt
/docker/proxy/.csr

# ---- everything else inside docker/ ----
# Any file not covered by a rule above (a clean docker-compose.yaml, nginx.conf,
# healthcheck.py, redis.conf that had nothing to redact, etc.) is tracked as
# its real, unmodified self by default. Per-service .gitignore files (one per
# service directory, generated by sanitize_service_files.py --write when it
# finds something, or added by hand for a known case like
# gitlab-runner/config.toml) are what carve out the specific exceptions.
